</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report AdvBench results in Table <a href="#A3.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Evaluation on AdvBench ‣ C.3 Additional Dataset and Metrics ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments ‣ Toward Safer Diffusion Language Models: Discovery and Mitigation of Priming Vulnerability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and Table <a href="#A3.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Evaluation on AdvBench ‣ C.3 Additional Dataset and Metrics ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments ‣ Toward Safer Diffusion Language Models: Discovery and Mitigation of Priming Vulnerability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.
Each table includes safety evaluations from three evaluators: GPT-4o, the guardrail model, and keyword matching.
Across AdvBench, RA consistently demonstrates high robustness.
Compared with JBB-Behaviors, AdvBench tends to yield lower ASR.
This reflects that JBB-Behaviors prompts are more adversarially crafted,
whereas AdvBench prompts are more direct and thus easier for models to refuse.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>Training Cost and Reward Curve</h3>

<div id="A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we report training time and learning curves for Recovery Alignment.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Compute Time.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report training time under the default configuration in Table <a href="#A3.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Compute Time. ‣ C.4 Training Cost and Reward Curve ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiments ‣ Toward Safer Diffusion Language Models: Discovery and Mitigation of Priming Vulnerability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.
We use the default training setting, the same as Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Experiments ‣ Toward Safer Diffusion Language Models: Discovery and Mitigation of Priming Vulnerability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
Training a single model for 2,500 steps on four GPUs takes about 16 hours. Because RA generates and evaluates multiple candidate responses at each step, its computational cost is higher than supervised methods such as SFT and DPO.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span>Training cost of Recovery Alignment.</figcaption>
